1
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:18,479
COMMENTARY:
The Fly came to me...

2
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,239
...from Mel Brooks, the producer.

3
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:22,471
He was working with Stuart Cornfeld.

4
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,632
And it was a script
that I had seen before...

5
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,156
...and was interested in somewhat,
but was very distracted...

6
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,196
...because I was working on
Total Recall with Dino De Laurentiis.

7
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:36,749
And I just wanna say
that this credit...

8
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:39,878
...this title moment...

9
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,789
...the sort of fluttering
of the words "The Fly"...

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,120
...was something I saw in a teaser trailer
that was done to promote the film.

11
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:52,432
And I liked it so much, I decided to
incorporate it into the head credits...

12
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,480
...because it wasn't something
we had initially thought of doing.

13
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:04,070
But the script for The Fly that I read
had been written by Chuck Pogue.

14
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:08,757
And it had a really wonderful rethinking
of the original movie...

15
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:11,230
...the Vincent Price movie.

16
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,269
The DNA rethink...

17
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:18,957
...of why a man could turn into
a sort of half-fly...

18
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:22,118
...and the sequence of physical events
that led up to it...

19
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:26,877
...were very brilliantly rethought
by Chuck Pogue.

20
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:31,472
But I wasn't very happy
with the characters...

21
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:34,154
...and I think they were
a little bit too close, in a way...

22
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:38,200
...to the characters in the original
short story by George Langelaan...

23
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:40,795
...who was, I think, a Belgian writer...

24
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:44,191
...whose story was published
in Playboy in 1957 ...

25
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:48,797
...and republished many times
after that. A very popular story.

26
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,632
So my contribution
to the rewriting of the script...

27
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:00,634
...was basically the characters
and the dialogue.

28
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,031
Which sounds like a lot, but it
wasn't everything. There was a lot...

29
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,716
...that attracted me to Chuck's script.

30
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:12,633
And felt, in fact, so much like me
that I was kind of surprised.

31
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:16,470
I'd have never-- I'd never really seen
a script that had so many things in it...

32
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:18,836
...that felt like me to me.

33
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,755
So I was very interested
to see where it would lead...

34
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:26,151
...and it led to Jeff Goldblum...

35
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:31,919
...and the woman that was his girlfriend
at the time, Geena Davis.

36
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:35,755
I auditioned a lot of actresses
for the role.

37
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,313
And I felt that because
Jeff's presence as Seth Brundle...

38
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:44,269
...the eccentricity, the charisma...

39
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:47,990
...and not to mention the fact
that Jeff is very tall, about 6'5"...

40
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:52,950
...really wanted to find an actress who
would match him in all of those ways.

41
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:58,069
And it seemed like kismet
that he should be...

42
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,311
His girlfriend should be Geena.

43
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,155
And it took a while before we came
around to saying to ourselves...

44
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:09,394
...and I mean me and Jeff, "Should we
consider Geena Davis to play this role?"

45
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,159
She'd mostly done comedies.

46
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:16,110
And the idea of a woman
who was tall, beautiful, sexy...

47
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:20,996
...and also very funny and eccentric,
well, that really attracted me.

48
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:24,158
And I felt that she was
a perfect match for Jeff.

49
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:26,550
VERONICA:
You're not a very good drunk.

50
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:30,110
COMMENTARY:
The problem, really...

51
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:33,636
...in working with a couple
who were so close...

52
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,997
...and had been together for
quite some time, was that Geena...

53
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:40,152
...who is an adept mimic...

54
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,436
...she basically would do Jeff.

55
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:48,436
I mean, she was like Jeff
in her linguistic rhythm...

56
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,517
...her speech rhythms
and her body language.

57
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,638
Because Jeff has a very strange...

58
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:58,112
...kind of infectious way
of speaking and moving.

59
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:01,995
And Geena was like his sister...

60
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:04,555
...more than a lover
that he'd just met.

61
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:09,670
And one of the things that we had to do
was to disconnect Geena from Jeff...

62
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,194
...for the sake of the movie...

63
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:14,398
...in terms of just her presence,
and her speech rhythms and so on.

64
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:17,393
I remember, she said, "Oh."
I said, "Geena, you're doing Jeff.

65
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:19,597
You're saying that line
the way Jeff would say it."

66
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:23,191
She said, "Oh, right. Do it
the Geena way, not the Jeff way."

67
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:29,078
And that's the kind of fun
that we had on the set. And it was fun.

68
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:37,636
Jeff, as you can see...

69
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,952
...is a very adept
piano player and singer.

70
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:45,717
And shortly after this shot, Jeff and
Geena did a duet for the crew together...

71
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:49,760
...with Jeff playing, and they sang
beautifully together. So it was a--

72
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,231
I could see where the togetherness
would come in their relationship.

73
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,358
But we had to make it fresh. We had to
separate them at the beginning...

74
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,113
...so that there was a seduction.

75
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:00,391
A mutual seduction.

76
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:03,791
And only gradually
towards the end of the movie...

77
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:06,349
...allowed them to become closer.

78
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:11,314
The telepod, and there it is
in all its glory...

79
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:16,998
Well, the first designs looked kind
of like glass showers, you know.

80
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:20,551
They were just-- Or telephone booths.

81
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:24,600
And as they were, in a way,
in the original movie of The Fly.

82
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:29,709
And I found that kind of,
hm, boring. And...

83
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,759
But we couldn't come up with what
the look for the telepods should be...

84
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:39,517
...until I opened my garage door
and showed my production designer...

85
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:43,719
...Carol Spier, my Ducati
single-cylinder motorcycle...

86
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:46,070
...a 450 Desmo.

87
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:48,476
And I said, "You know,
it should be more machine-y.

88
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:52,469
It should have cooling fins, for example.
It should have camshafts or something."

89
00:05:52,680 --> 00:05:56,230
And I thought, "Well, let me show you."
Well, in about two minutes...

90
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:01,076
...she and her crew had taken
the engine out of my motorcycle...

91
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:04,557
...which was a vintage
Ducati single-cylinder bike...

92
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:07,951
...and taken it off to their own lab...

93
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:12,557
...where they re-created it
in a giant scale, turned it upside down.

94
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:17,078
But basically, all "Ducatisti,"
Ducati enthusiasts...

95
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,993
...of that particular
Italian motorcycle...

96
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:24,119
...recognized the telepod
as a Ducati cylinder head.

97
00:06:24,280 --> 00:06:27,113
More than a cylinder head, it's
actually the whole cylinder structure.

98
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:29,510
So that was--
That's the basis of that design.

99
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,792
Italian design.
You can't beat it, it's great.

100
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:51,157
Geena, of course, and Jeff
knew very well how to play together.

101
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,870
Flirt and to--
Just the sexual innuendos and so on.

102
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,870
And Jeff, I couldn't believe my luck
when I found him for this role...

103
00:07:00,080 --> 00:07:05,837
...because he had that unique
combination of geekiness...

104
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,391
...with believable,
credible intelligence...

105
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:16,398
...and yet the potential to become an
incredibly physical, athletic presence...

106
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:21,993
...which the role required, and which
we'll certainly see more of later.

107
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:26,790
So this is-- To me, this was a marriage
made in heaven, between me and Jeff.

108
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:30,430
That was our fusion. We were
really fusing like Brundlefly...

109
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:32,352
...because you know, I really--

110
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,876
When you write a script
and you are directing a movie...

111
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:40,278
...there's a sense in which a director
fuses with his leading actors.

112
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:44,274
Not just the actor,
but the actress as well.

113
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:47,114
You sort of become them.
You have to become them.

114
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:50,272
You get-- You really become attuned
to the rhythms and so on.

115
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:53,671
And it continues on past
the shooting of the movie.

116
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:56,639
It continues into the editing,
when the actor's long gone.

117
00:07:56,800 --> 00:08:00,077
You're still dealing with that actor's
verbal rhythms and physical rhythms...

118
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:03,835
...and so on, because you're trying to
make them work in an editing way.

119
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:09,837
You really get into the nervous system
of your actors when you're directing.

120
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,029
And it is like a fusion,
a sort of Brundlefly fusion.

121
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:16,829
VERONICA:
If I had a Rolex.

122
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,236
You're missing the point.
Look.

123
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:28,599
Teleportation?

124
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:32,799
COMMENTARY:
The original story of "The Fly"...

125
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:34,997
...was not really a great story...

126
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:37,959
...in the sense of the quality
of its writing and so on.

127
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:43,559
I mean, Mangelaan is-- Or Langelaan
is not really a very well-known writer...

128
00:08:43,720 --> 00:08:45,836
...except for that short story.

129
00:08:46,680 --> 00:08:52,312
But it did have that basic concept
that is so stunning and so...

130
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,869
I guess you would call it
high-concept now.

131
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:57,759
It wasn't when he wrote it, obviously.

132
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:01,390
But for a little story, for a sort
of awkwardly written little story...

133
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:04,518
...it certainly had a huge impact
when it came out...

134
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:08,310
...and spawned, and that's
the right word, quite a few movies...

135
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:12,269
...and will probably spawn
a few more before it's done.

136
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:15,195
VERONICA:
What happened?

137
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,960
You get it but you can't handle it.

138
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:21,158
Um, your stocking...

139
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:25,109
COMMENTARY: The name "Brundle"
I took from a Formula One racer...

140
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:27,556
...and a car racer
named Martin Brundle.

141
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:30,872
A lot of the names
that I invent for my characters...

142
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:35,511
...come from the annals
of motor racing.

143
00:09:36,480 --> 00:09:38,437
I don't know why.
Just for some reason.

144
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:40,910
I'm a big enthusiast of motor racing.

145
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:44,636
And even though Brundle...

146
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:47,195
...as he's already explained,
gets motion sick.

147
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,671
And that's one of the reasons
that he's invented teleportation...

148
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:52,917
...is that he won't have to
get into a vehicle.

149
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:55,351
VERONICA:
How could you do this alone?

150
00:09:56,040 --> 00:09:57,792
I don't work alone.

151
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,469
There's stuff in there I don't
understand. I'm really...

152
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:02,393
...a systems manager.
I farm out...

153
00:10:02,640 --> 00:10:07,794
COMMENTARY: The idea of having
Seth's home and lab...

154
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:11,794
...in one place, in a warehouse,
in a kind of loft space...

155
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:14,600
...seems obvious now,
and you've seen it a million times...

156
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:17,229
...and we've seen a million movies
in which people...

157
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:19,277
...live in lofts and warehouses
and so on...

158
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:21,431
...but at the time,
it was really rather unusual.

159
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:23,477
There weren't too many movies
before The Fly...

160
00:10:23,640 --> 00:10:27,713
...in which the lead character
lived in his workspace...

161
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:33,876
...and that that workspace was a kind of
old-- A semi-abandoned warehouse.

162
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:37,513
BRUNDLE:
What's that?

163
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:39,791
COMMENTARY:
And, of course, it worked perfectly...

164
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:41,553
...for the theatricality of the piece.

165
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:45,196
I've often said that The Fly
could be a play...

166
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:49,433
...and in fact, I'm working right now
with Howard Shore...

167
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:51,511
...on an opera version of The Fly.

168
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:56,038
Because it's really-- It's almost--
We knew from the very beginning...

169
00:10:56,200 --> 00:11:01,878
...that there was a theatricality
and an operatic aspect to the movie.

170
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:05,317
That it was not-- Even though
it's very-- A very small cast...

171
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:08,996
...it really is basically three
characters in one space.

172
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:11,551
Four, if you count the baboon.

173
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:16,430
VERONICA: But you told it to me.
-I suppose I did but--

174
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:18,553
-Sure did.
-Now wait a minute.

175
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:22,114
COMMENTARY: So it's not an epic,
but it's more intimate than that.

176
00:11:22,280 --> 00:11:24,237
But there is an operatic element to it.

177
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:28,314
And that's what we went for
with the music.

178
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:34,677
It's not exactly over-the-top,
but it doesn't restrain itself either.

179
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:40,311
And I have to say that my response
to seeing this movie...

180
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:44,474
...after not having seen it
since 1986 or 7...

181
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:46,392
...in order to do this commentary...

182
00:11:46,560 --> 00:11:50,872
...I was really struck by
how disturbing a film it is, I have to say.

183
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,639
And how emotional it is.

184
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:55,235
Certainly, that was something
that attracted people to it...

185
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:57,789
...who normally would not be
interested in a horror film...

186
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:01,351
...especially one that
gets relatively gory.

187
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:04,354
There were always--
There was always a lot of...

188
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,880
...positive response to the love story,
to the triangle you see here...

189
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:12,351
...with John Getz,
playing Stathis Borans.

190
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:16,918
And it was something that, I guess at
a certain point after making the movie...

191
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:18,798
...I kind of lost track of myself.

192
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:22,112
And it struck me
on seeing it just recently...

193
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:25,392
...how right the people were
who had that reaction...

194
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:30,396
...and how disturbing
and emotional the film is.

195
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:32,597
It really does work as a love story.

196
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:39,277
John Getz, I saw him in Blood Simple,
the Coen brothers film.

197
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:41,875
And I loved what he had done
in that movie...

198
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:44,600
...and was very excited
to get him for this.

199
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:47,679
Just the presence, the voice,
the arrogance...

200
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:50,593
...but also the vulnerability
and the sarcasm and so on...

201
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:52,956
...mean,
which this character required.

202
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:56,112
Someone who could really
stand up to Jeff's strength...

203
00:12:56,280 --> 00:12:58,840
...with his own
different kind of strength.

204
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,280
BRUNDLE:
You didn't waste time.

205
00:13:04,120 --> 00:13:06,191
I'm not getting any younger.

206
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:10,870
He wasn't impressed by your tape?

207
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:13,635
He thinks you're a con man.

208
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:15,074
COMMENTARY:
This is, I think...

209
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:17,595
...the only version of The Fly
that had a love affair...

210
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:21,276
...that developed before your eyes.
Both the short story and the early film...

211
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:24,319
...and Chuck Pogue's original script...

212
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:28,519
...had the scientist married
when we meet him.

213
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:31,633
And so it was a sort of
a more domestic...

214
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:35,031
...established marital situation
at the beginning.

215
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:38,556
I really wanted
there to be more of a process...

216
00:13:38,720 --> 00:13:41,553
...more of the excitement
of a seduction...

217
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:44,360
...and a love affair developing.

218
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:49,953
And I wanted Brundle to be
very articulate about what he does...

219
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:52,231
...because I wanted him
to be very articulate...

220
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:56,280
...about what was happening to him
as the film progressed.

221
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:59,034
And in other versions of The Fly...

222
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:01,714
...including the script
that I originally read...

223
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:04,799
...the scientist loses his voice
very early on...

224
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:08,191
...relatively early on in the movie,
as he transforms.

225
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:10,874
And therefore is limited...

226
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:14,237
...in terms of what he can express,
how articulate he can be.

227
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:16,311
And I think in the original movie...

228
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:18,750
...he can only type some words
on an old typewriter...

229
