we could take a holiday.

465
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:46,192
COMMENTARY:
I did a photo with the baboon...

466
00:29:46,360 --> 00:29:47,475
...where I was kind of--

467
00:29:47,640 --> 00:29:51,270
We were kind of kissing each other. But
I was well aware that at any moment...

468
00:29:51,440 --> 00:29:56,116
...he might try to rip my face off,
and could probably do it quite easily.

469
00:29:57,320 --> 00:30:01,837
He was scary, but beautiful.
And performed very well.

470
00:30:02,200 --> 00:30:04,350
BRUNDLE:
Is this a romance we're having?

471
00:30:06,160 --> 00:30:08,470
Yeah. Could be a romance.

472
00:30:09,720 --> 00:30:10,869
Come here.

473
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:18,471
I have a great idea.

474
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:25,200
You like Chinese food?

475
00:30:26,160 --> 00:30:27,434
Yes.

476
00:30:29,320 --> 00:30:31,436
We'll have a romantic dinner here.

477
00:30:36,480 --> 00:30:38,198
COMMENTARY:
I love the kind of invention...

478
00:30:38,360 --> 00:30:40,397
...that goes on
sort of as a subtext in a movie...

479
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:44,713
...and the invention of Particle Magazine
was something I really enjoyed.

480
00:30:44,920 --> 00:30:46,638
And this cover art.

481
00:30:47,560 --> 00:30:48,789
BRUNDLE:
Yeah, I'll wait.

482
00:30:52,480 --> 00:30:53,800
BRUNDLE:
What's that?

483
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:55,399
[VERONICA SIGHS]

484
00:30:55,840 --> 00:30:57,672
COMMENTARY:
The idea that you might have...

485
00:30:57,840 --> 00:30:59,672
...a People magazine
or a TIME magazine...

486
00:30:59,840 --> 00:31:03,754
...but for physics, for advanced physics.
I kind of enjoyed that idea.

487
00:31:04,840 --> 00:31:06,877
I'm not sure that it exists.

488
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:12,509
I guess Scientific American
is the closest that comes.

489
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:16,635
It's hard to believe this movie
was made 20 years ago...

490
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:18,837
...until you see phones like that.

491
00:31:19,040 --> 00:31:22,635
Then you are aware
of how things have changed.

492
00:31:23,080 --> 00:31:24,832
VERONICA:
I have to scrape it off my shoe...

493
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:27,071
...and get rid of it, once and for all.

494
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:31,554
Hi.

495
00:31:31,800 --> 00:31:32,915
[DOOR CLOSES]

496
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:38,118
COMMENTARY:
This movie was made before--

497
00:31:38,280 --> 00:31:39,509
Really before there was CGI.

498
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:43,753
Everything in this movie was done
on an optical bench, not in a computer.

499
00:31:43,920 --> 00:31:46,389
No frames of this film
went in and out of computers...

500
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:50,155
...and that's almost unthinkable these
days, especially for a sci-fi movie.

501
00:31:50,360 --> 00:31:53,796
But it was all done the old way,
the handmade way.

502
00:31:53,960 --> 00:31:55,837
The Seth Brundle way, I guess.

503
00:31:57,480 --> 00:31:59,437
VERONICA:
You told me there was no story.

504
00:31:59,600 --> 00:32:01,113
You said Brundle was a con man.

505
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:04,798
I've decided to trust
your journalistic instincts.

506
00:32:05,040 --> 00:32:07,953
Thanks very much.
But this is not your story.

507
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:09,639
It's mine.

508
00:32:09,920 --> 00:32:11,149
Says who?

509
00:32:11,400 --> 00:32:14,711
I sent you to the Bartok party
to see what you could find.

510
00:32:14,960 --> 00:32:16,951
Your discovery is my discovery.

511
00:32:17,640 --> 00:32:20,393
I have a lot of background on Brundle.

512
00:32:20,600 --> 00:32:23,558
COMMENTARY:
I guess this would be a sort of...

513
00:32:23,720 --> 00:32:28,794
...sexual-harassment-in-the-workplace
situation now.

514
00:32:28,960 --> 00:32:30,871
The employer and the employee.

515
00:32:32,600 --> 00:32:35,433
I think that was in the air,
even in 1986.

516
00:32:35,600 --> 00:32:40,470
But it wasn't as...
Had not been as codified...

517
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:44,918
...and as embedded in law and so on
as it has been since then.

518
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:49,559
STATHIS: Look, just keep me informed.
All right?

519
00:32:50,160 --> 00:32:51,434
As a friend?

520
00:32:52,480 --> 00:32:54,551
As a professional confidante?

521
00:32:56,640 --> 00:32:57,914
That's all?

522
00:33:01,040 --> 00:33:02,997
I don't want you
to disappear from my life.

523
00:33:03,840 --> 00:33:05,592
COMMENTARY:
But here he says something...

524
00:33:05,760 --> 00:33:08,673
...that's meant to be
true and touching.

525
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:13,914
Underneath all the sort of--
The bullying and the arrogance...

526
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:18,711
...Is a man who is desperate to keep
this woman in his life any way he can.

527
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:22,236
And he really cares
for her and about her.

528
00:33:22,440 --> 00:33:25,114
They do have a past together.

529
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:27,234
VERONICA:
As always.

530
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:32,759
Couldn't disappoint you.

531
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:36,920
Residue means your old boyfriend,
doesn't it?

532
00:33:37,120 --> 00:33:39,111
COMMENTARY:
And here's another relationship.

533
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:42,391
Seth and his baboon, of course.

534
00:33:42,720 --> 00:33:45,234
BRUNDLE:
"From the desk of Stathis Borans."

535
00:33:46,080 --> 00:33:48,549
How about, "Under the desk
of Stathis Borans"?

536
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:53,077
COMMENTARY: Now, given that
you can't really train a baboon...

537
00:33:54,480 --> 00:33:56,994
...this is not an animal
that will do tricks for you.

538
00:33:57,200 --> 00:34:00,670
We had to find ways
to get him to perform.

539
00:34:00,840 --> 00:34:02,831
And...

540
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:09,360
In this case,
we actually attached a living fly...

541
00:34:09,520 --> 00:34:12,592
...to, in essence, a tiny fishing line...

542
00:34:12,800 --> 00:34:17,431
...made out of-- I think it was
filament from light bulbs.

543
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:20,399
So that was basically invisible,
because, as I say, in those days...

544
00:34:20,600 --> 00:34:25,151
...you couldn't remove
a line or a wire...

545
00:34:25,320 --> 00:34:29,598
...by going into a computer. You would
have to hand-paint it out, every frame.

546
00:34:29,800 --> 00:34:31,598
You couldn't do it in a computer.

547
00:34:31,760 --> 00:34:38,109
So we had to sort of
play that fly, literally, on a...

548
00:34:38,760 --> 00:34:42,037
With a fishing rod
around the nose of the baboon...

549
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:43,913
...so that he could respond to the fly.

550
00:34:44,120 --> 00:34:46,077
And that was our
first moment where...

551
00:34:46,240 --> 00:34:49,437
...a fly, any fly, makes his appearance.

552
00:34:50,320 --> 00:34:52,880
Of course, he becomes a star now.

553
00:35:12,200 --> 00:35:14,840
The telepod ended up
traveling around the world, actually...

554
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,514
...to various exhibits of props
from my movies.

555
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:20,752
It went to Tokyo.
It went to Thessalonica...

556
00:35:21,560 --> 00:35:22,959
...in Greece. It went to Paris.

557
00:35:23,160 --> 00:35:27,791
And it was at the Royal Ontario Museum
in Toronto, as well.

558
00:35:28,880 --> 00:35:32,396
It had to keep being rebuilt because, of
course, props are never built to last.

559
00:35:32,560 --> 00:35:34,915
There's no reason to make them last.

560
00:35:35,080 --> 00:35:37,071
And so they're usually not...

561
00:35:37,280 --> 00:35:40,033
...sealed against the weather
and so on.

562
00:35:40,280 --> 00:35:44,831
And this telepod ended up
in Carol Spier's backyard, decaying...

563
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:48,834
...until suddenly it was refurbished
and sent off to Paris.

564
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:51,554
A new life.

565
00:35:51,720 --> 00:35:54,109
A transformation, in fact.

566
00:35:59,280 --> 00:36:02,830
Now, here we begin to get into
the physicality of Seth.

567
00:36:03,760 --> 00:36:06,479
A nerd, a geek with muscles.

568
00:36:06,840 --> 00:36:08,592
And a very athletic body.

569
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:11,832
BRUNDLE:
Now, you tell me.

570
00:36:12,440 --> 00:36:14,351
Am I different somehow?

571
00:36:14,920 --> 00:36:16,433
"Is it live or is it Memorex?"

572
00:36:16,600 --> 00:36:18,477
COMMENTARY:
And there's the baboon mugging...

573
00:36:18,640 --> 00:36:20,551
...giving me-- Giving me some--

574
00:36:20,760 --> 00:36:23,559
Good look towards the camera.

575
00:36:39,040 --> 00:36:42,078
This hallway was on a location.

576
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:46,831
And then the interior was a set
that we controlled.

577
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:50,231
And then we built a bit of the hallway
so that when we opened the door...

578
00:36:50,400 --> 00:36:53,995
...you could see it.
But now we're, of course, on our set.

579
00:37:08,480 --> 00:37:11,916
So unlike the original movie
of The Fly...

580
00:37:12,080 --> 00:37:14,594
...he comes through
the teleportation...

581
00:37:15,040 --> 00:37:16,678
. first of all...

582
00:37:16,840 --> 00:37:21,277
...out of love and frustration and
drunkenness, not just scientific curiosity.

583
00:37:21,440 --> 00:37:25,479
So there's an emotional element there
that there wasn't quite in the original.

584
00:37:27,960 --> 00:37:29,951
And then also...

585
00:37:30,160 --> 00:37:33,118
...we are surprised to see that he seems
to not be transformed at all.

586
00:37:33,280 --> 00:37:38,070
He's not changed. His head is
not a giant fly's head. And his...

587
00:37:38,240 --> 00:37:41,915
One of his hands
is not a giant fly claw.

588
00:37:42,080 --> 00:37:45,471
It always bugged me,
if I can make the pun...

589
00:37:45,640 --> 00:37:49,270
...that, in the original,
the fly's head...

590
00:37:49,960 --> 00:37:53,635
...was human-sized, or actually fit
over a human's head, of course.

591
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:58,520
Because there was no scientific
or logical reason why...

592
00:37:58,680 --> 00:38:02,674
...if the head swap happened, that
the fly's head should suddenly be bigger.

593
00:38:02,880 --> 00:38:05,759
I didn't-- There was no rationale for that
that worked for me.

594
00:38:05,920 --> 00:38:07,672
There was no way to make that--

595
00:38:07,840 --> 00:38:12,277
And even as a kid,
seeing the movie in 1957, which I did--

596
00:38:12,440 --> 00:38:16,718
And it had a big impact, of course,
when it came out, that movie.

597
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:20,191
Even though I think its campiness
and its flaws and on certain levels...

598
00:38:20,360 --> 00:38:23,830
...were obvious even then to a kid.

599
00:38:24,480 --> 00:38:26,869
That bothered me. There were two
things that bothered me.

600
00:38:27,040 --> 00:38:29,350
One was the proportions
of the fly head.

601
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:32,911
There was no way it could be big
instead of just fly-sized.

602
00:38:33,080 --> 00:38:35,515
And the second thing was
there was a famous shot...

603
00:38:35,680 --> 00:38:37,159
...that's the fly's point of view.

604
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:42,119
And it was a mosaic.
It was identical shots of his wife.

605
00:38:42,280 --> 00:38:46,433
You know, sort of a hundred of them.
The fly looking at his screaming wife.

606
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:51,550
We all know that insect eyes
have facets...

607
00:38:51,720 --> 00:38:55,554
...unlike human eyes. Many facets,
hundreds of them, thousands of them.

608
00:38:55,720 --> 00:38:57,757
But that was also wrong.

609
00:38:57,960 --> 00:39:01,191
Because as a junior entomologist,
as I was at the time...

610
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:04,239
...I knew that that's not
the way flies saw.

611
00:39:04,400 --> 00:39:10,749
The idea is that there's no functional
value to a repetition of the same image.

612
00:39:10,920 --> 00:39:12,479
That doesn't help an insect at all,
like a dragonfly...

613
00:39:13,640 --> 00:39:16,553
...for example,
which has almost 360-degree eyes.

614
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:19,838
Each one of those little facets
would be a different...

615
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:22,594
...piece of a puzzle of a big picture.

616
00:39:22,760 --> 00:39:24,831
So it was wrong.
It was scientifically wrong.

617
00:39:25,000 --> 00:39:27,674
And it didn't make any sense.
And that bugged me too as a kid.

618
00:39:27,840 --> 00:39:32,391
I was determined that we would not
make those kind of mistakes.

619
00:39:33,120 --> 00:39:36,192
That there would be no kids like me
watching my version of The Fly...

620
00:39:36,400 --> 00:39:39,995
...who would say, "Yeah, but that's
not the way insects" whatever.

621
00:39:41,440 --> 00:39:44,876
Well, because of
Chuck Pogue's rethinking...

622
00:39:45,040 --> 00:39:47,190
...of the sort of the DNA situation...

623
00:39:47,360 --> 00:39:49,556
...which, of course,
was not known in 1957--

624
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:51,239
DNA was not...

625
00:39:51,400 --> 00:39:55,234
Had only really just begun
to be understood at that time...

626
00:39:55,400 --> 00:39:58,074
...and not really by the general public.

627
00:39:58,240 --> 00:40:02,711
So the rethinking of the movie
as being a gradual transformation...

628
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:06,191
...because of the fusing
of the DNA of the fly...

629
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:10,030
...and the DNA of Brundle
meant that I could avoid...

630
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:12,794
...automatically would avoid...

631
00:40:12,960 --> 00:40:14,837
...sort of the crude errors that--

632
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:18,152
As I thought of them
when I saw the original Fly.

633
00:40:18,320 --> 00:40:21,278
And the original Fly was--
The movie...

634
00:40:21,440 --> 00:40:23,909
...was set in Montreal,
for some reason.

635
00:40:24,080 --> 00:40:27,710
And the characters all had French names.
It was quite odd.

636
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:29,399
They weren't French-Canadian names.

637
00:40:29,560 --> 00:40:32,313
They didn't have French-Canadian
accents. They had French accents...

638
00:40:32,480 --> 00:40:34,517
...which-- Anybody who knows Quebec
knows that...

639
00:40:34,720 --> 00:40:37,109
...those are two different kinds
of accents.

640
00:40:37,280 --> 00:40:41,797
So for a Canadian watching the original
Fly, there were a lot of oddities.

641
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:44,913
I don't know how it appeared to
Americans or to anyone else...

642
00:40:45,080 --> 00:40:49,995
...but it certainly seemed wrong
in a strange way. In that strange way.

643
00:41:07,840 --> 00:41:09,478
Now, Jeff...

644
00:41:09,640 --> 00:41:11,631
...who, as I say, is about 6'5"...

645
00:41:11,840 --> 00:41:15,151
...could do the beginnings
of these gymnastic moves.

646
00:41:16,840 --> 00:41:19,912
And he was very impressive
to the gymnasts...

647
00:41:20,080 --> 00:41:22,117
...who we brought in
to do the rest of the move.

648
00:41:22,280 --> 00:41:25,159
Now, this is a gymnast
who's about 5'8" doing that.

649
00:41:25,320 --> 00:41:29,712
But, of course, that's Jeff right there.
Gymnasts are not 6'5".

650
00:41:29,880 --> 00:41:33,635
The proportions of muscles to height
and so on dictate that...

651
00:41:33,840 --> 00:41:39,916
...most gold-medal-winning gymnasts
are about 54", 5'6", 5'6" at maximum.

652
00:41:40,120 --> 00:41:43,556
We found a couple of gymnasts
who were more like 5'8"...

653
00:41:43,720 --> 00:41:49,875
...and they were considered huge
in terms of competing gymnasts.

654
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:52,477
But they were nonetheless
very impressed...

655
00:41:52,640 --> 00:41:56,713
...with the moves that Jeff could do,
which of course helped me...

656
00:41:56,880 --> 00:42:02,000
...because the fact that he could do the
beginnings of these moves on his own...

657
00:42:02,160 --> 00:42:06,154
...helped with the illusion
of these stunt--

658
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:10,115
Well, they're actually
competing gymnasts...

659
00:42:10,280 --> 00:42:11,759
...standing in for Jeff.

660
00:42:12,400 --> 00:42:15,950
It takes years of training to be able
to do these things.

661
00:42:16,120 --> 00:42:18,350
And also, since it's a movie...

662
00:42:18,520 --> 00:42:20,830
...I had them do it over and over
and over again...

663
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:24,152
...to the point where they were starting
to not be able to do the moves.

664
00:42:24,320 --> 00:42:27,039
And, of course, they say,
"You know, when we do competitions...

665
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:31,273
...you're basically training for months
and years, and you do it once."

666
00:42:31,480 --> 00:42:34,199
You know, you do your routine once,
when you do the competition.

667
00:42:34,360 --> 00:42:37,193
You don't do it over and over.
You don't do take 12.

668
00:42:37,360 --> 00:42:41,991
So I burned through a couple
of gymnasts getting the routine down.

669
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:48,719
And I wanted it not to just be...

670
00:42:48,880 --> 00:42:52,999
...strength moves, not just being able
to lift heavy weights and so on, which I--

671
00:42:53,160 --> 00:42:55,993
I wanted him to show agility...

672
00:42:56,160 --> 00:43:00,040
...because flies are fantastic fliers.

673
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:02,356
And, of course, I couldn't have Jeff
flying around.

674
00:43:02,520 --> 00:43:05,160
So to me the alternative was...

675
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:09,310
...that he should become incredibly
strong in proportion to his body...

676
00:43:09,480 --> 00:43:10,800
...as insects are...

677
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:14,959
...and that he should be
nimble and agile...

678
00:43:15,280 --> 00:43:18,591
...as flies are,
in terms of the way they fly.

679
00:43:22,280 --> 00:43:23,873
This sequence was shot...

680
00:43:24,040 --> 00:43:26,998
...in the Kensington Market
section of Toronto.

681
00:43:27,160 --> 00:43:31,757
That gentleman in the tuque
was our props master on the movie.

682
00:43:38,160 --> 00:43:42,711
You're constantly bribing your crew
with little cameos.

683
00:43:48,520 --> 00:43:51,512
And it's probably
the most traditional love affair...

684
00:43:51,680 --> 00:43:55,036
...that I've ever depicted in any
of my movies, this part right here.

685
00:43:55,200 --> 00:43:57,555
But, of course, we know
that under the surface...

686
00:43:57,720 --> 00:43:59,393
...strange things are happening.

687
00:43:59,560 --> 00:44:01,551
And here's the first indication--

688
00:44:02,040 --> 00:44:04,350
BRUNDLE: --about what
I'm feeling and why. I'm thinking...

689
00:44:04,600 --> 00:44:06,910
COMMENTARY: --of the bad part
of what might be happening.

690
00:44:07,080 --> 00:44:08,912
BRUNDLE:
--and reassembled--

691
00:44:09,080 --> 00:44:10,593
It's like filtering coffee.

692
00:44:10,760 --> 00:44:13,400
COMMENTARY: Because after all,
flies are speedy, aren't they?

693
00:44:14,520 --> 00:44:17,512
And Jeff is very speedy in this scene.

694
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:23,795
The idea of fly DNA
as an amphetamine was part of...

695
00:44:25,520 --> 00:44:27,431
...what I liked about the rethinking...

696
00:44:29,120 --> 00:44:30,758
...of the original transformation...

697
00:44:30,920 --> 00:44:32,433
...which was...

698
00:44:32,640 --> 00:44:35,314
...quite physical,
quite straightforward, really.

699
