the movie was just about...

933
00:59:03,880 --> 00:59:07,555
...the normal process of aging.

934
00:59:07,720 --> 00:59:09,791
And, of course,
possibly disease as well.

935
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:13,197
But it wasn't specifically about AIDS.
That certainly wasn't in my mind.

936
00:59:13,360 --> 00:59:14,430
And I thought that it was really...

937
00:59:15,760 --> 00:59:19,719
...much more about the inevitability
of deterioration and death.

938
00:59:21,920 --> 00:59:25,993
And that that's where the movie
got a lot of its power from...

939
00:59:26,160 --> 00:59:30,552
...was just that it's--
Anybody can relate to it, really.

940
00:59:31,120 --> 00:59:33,270
Despite the fact that...

941
00:59:33,480 --> 00:59:39,829
...the circumstances in the movie
are fantastical and sci-fi and so on...

942
00:59:40,560 --> 00:59:43,473
...the emotional
and very physical reality...

943
00:59:44,240 --> 00:59:48,598
...is absolutely accurate and true.
And what's more, inevitable.

944
00:59:49,240 --> 00:59:54,155
So it's hard to escape
that vibe of The Fly.

945
00:59:54,320 --> 00:59:57,312
That's one of the things that I found
very disturbing when I watched it.

946
00:59:57,480 --> 01:00:01,838
You know, I was surprised that I was
quite shaky after I watched this movie.

947
01:00:02,000 --> 01:00:04,196
It really surprised me.

948
01:00:04,680 --> 01:00:07,513
Normally, it's very hard for me to react
to watching my own movies...

949
01:00:07,720 --> 01:00:09,950
...because I remember
everything that happened...

950
01:00:10,120 --> 01:00:12,031
...on the day that we shot every shot.

951
01:00:12,240 --> 01:00:14,675
And so it's a kind of
a mini-documentary to me...

952
01:00:14,840 --> 01:00:16,638
...about what I was doing that day.

953
01:00:16,800 --> 01:00:20,316
And that makes it very hard
to react to my own movies...

954
01:00:20,520 --> 01:00:22,796
...as though they're movies, basically.

955
01:00:22,960 --> 01:00:26,396
But I did manage to do that
with The Fly somehow...

956
01:00:26,560 --> 01:00:29,791
...even though I was also thinking
about all those other things.

957
01:00:30,040 --> 01:00:34,432
And I found it to be a very, as I say,
profoundly disturbing experience.

958
01:00:34,600 --> 01:00:37,194
And the reason, of course,
is that I'm older now...

959
01:00:37,360 --> 01:00:41,957
...I've experienced more of
the things that I was just anticipating...

960
01:00:42,120 --> 01:00:47,354
...in a kind of precocious understanding
20 years ago when I made this movie.

961
01:00:47,600 --> 01:00:53,232
And so, for me, this movie isn't really
about AIDS. It's about mortality...

962
01:00:53,440 --> 01:00:56,080
...and the way that we deal with it
and try to understand it...

963
01:00:56,240 --> 01:00:59,995
...and the philosophies and emotional
attitudes that we develop towards it.

964
01:01:00,160 --> 01:01:03,710
That's the existentialist in me
speaking here.

965
01:01:04,920 --> 01:01:08,197
I've often said that this movie,
if it were made as a straight drama...

966
01:01:08,360 --> 01:01:11,671
...would never have gotten made,
because it's really quite depressing.

967
01:01:11,840 --> 01:01:16,596
But because it's protected
by the trappings of the genre...

968
01:01:16,760 --> 01:01:20,879
...because it's a sci-fi horror movie,
all of that seems to be okay.

969
01:01:21,080 --> 01:01:24,436
But if you tried to sell somebody
on a movie about two attractive...

970
01:01:24,600 --> 01:01:26,750
...eccentric people who fall in love...

971
01:01:29,280 --> 01:01:32,636
...and then one of them contracts
a hideous, wasting disease...

972
01:01:32,800 --> 01:01:35,474
...and the other one watches
as he deteriorates...

973
01:01:35,640 --> 01:01:39,156
...and then helps him commit suicide at
the end, this is a tough sell for a movie.

974
01:01:39,360 --> 01:01:43,319
This is not something that would
probably get made very easily.

975
01:01:46,640 --> 01:01:49,075
But that is what the plot
of this movie is.

976
01:01:49,240 --> 01:01:52,437
And yet, it doesn't feel quite like that.

977
01:01:52,600 --> 01:01:56,480
Because of the sweetness and
eccentricity of the love story, partly...

978
01:01:56,640 --> 01:01:59,029
...and the attractiveness of it
and of the actors.

979
01:01:59,200 --> 01:02:02,670
And then also because
of the sci-fi underpinnings...

980
01:02:02,880 --> 01:02:06,032
...which are intriguing and provocative
and also fun.

981
01:02:06,920 --> 01:02:08,831
BRUNDLE:
I've been afraid to see you.

982
01:02:09,080 --> 01:02:13,631
Now I'm afraid not to.

983
01:02:13,880 --> 01:02:15,234
Are you at home?

984
01:02:15,480 --> 01:02:17,551
You don't know how right you were.

985
01:02:17,760 --> 01:02:19,433
I've gotten...

986
01:02:19,600 --> 01:02:21,637
...much, much worse.

987
01:02:21,800 --> 01:02:23,711
COMMENTARY:
But you really have to consider...

988
01:02:23,880 --> 01:02:24,995
...how many people...

989
01:02:25,160 --> 01:02:28,835
...have given themselves their own
death sentence in their bathrooms...

990
01:02:29,000 --> 01:02:33,153
...by discovering that thing in the
shower or in the bathtub or in the mirror.

991
01:02:33,320 --> 01:02:36,358
That's where the potency
of those scenes comes from.

992
01:02:51,840 --> 01:02:53,194
VERONICA:
Seth?

993
01:02:53,880 --> 01:02:54,915
I'm here.

994
01:02:55,160 --> 01:02:56,309
BRUNDLE:
Stop!

995
01:02:57,760 --> 01:02:58,989
Seth!

996
01:03:03,960 --> 01:03:08,636
COMMENTARY: And once again,
it was very important to have Seth...

997
01:03:08,800 --> 01:03:13,920
...be articulate about
what was going on with himself.

998
01:03:14,120 --> 01:03:16,475
As people--

999
01:03:16,640 --> 01:03:19,473
Well, we know of many people
who have written books about their--

1000
01:03:19,640 --> 01:03:22,871
The diseases that they've been
afflicted with that eventually kill them.

1001
01:03:23,040 --> 01:03:26,510
And some of those books are quite
brilliant and illuminating...

1002
01:03:26,680 --> 01:03:30,594
...even though they're always depressing
because of the ending.

1003
01:03:30,800 --> 01:03:33,599
And so I wanted this to have
some of that feeling.

1004
01:03:33,760 --> 01:03:36,195
That we would understand...

1005
01:03:36,360 --> 01:03:39,637
...both the intellectual
and emotional twists and turns...

1006
01:03:39,800 --> 01:03:41,996
...that he was going through
as he experienced this.

1007
01:03:42,160 --> 01:03:44,515
Then of course, what happens is
that the audience then...

1008
01:03:44,960 --> 01:03:47,554
...gets to experience it more as well.

1009
01:03:47,720 --> 01:03:51,839
It makes it that much more potent
and viscerally affecting.

1010
01:03:52,000 --> 01:03:54,753
BRUNDLE: --transmitter pod with
me that first time when I was alone.

1011
01:03:59,280 --> 01:04:00,793
COMMENTARY:
This movie won an Oscar...

1012
01:04:01,000 --> 01:04:03,560
...for makeup effects.

1013
01:04:03,720 --> 01:04:06,314
It's the only movie that I've done...

1014
01:04:06,480 --> 01:04:09,313
...that was nominated for an Oscar
of any kind, and it won.

1015
01:04:09,480 --> 01:04:11,278
It was quite fun to be there
on that day...

1016
01:04:11,440 --> 01:04:14,353
...even though not being personally
nominated myself...

1017
01:04:14,520 --> 01:04:16,318
...I was not sitting
on the ground floor...

1018
01:04:16,480 --> 01:04:19,233
...but was with my wife
up on the third or fourth floor...

1019
01:04:19,400 --> 01:04:22,233
...of the Dorothy Chandler Pavilion.
But it was still fun.

1020
01:04:23,600 --> 01:04:25,955
My first time at the Oscars.

1021
01:04:26,440 --> 01:04:29,717
BRUNDLE: I'm the offspring
of Brundle and a housefly.

1022
01:04:31,600 --> 01:04:34,797
Oh, God. Oh, God, Seth!

1023
01:04:35,040 --> 01:04:36,838
You look so pretty.

1024
01:04:37,840 --> 01:04:39,751
COMMENTARY:
But it was important for me not to--

1025
01:04:39,920 --> 01:04:42,833
Even in scenes like this,
to not lose sight of the fly-ness...

1026
01:04:43,040 --> 01:04:44,633
...of what was happening to him...

1027
01:04:44,800 --> 01:04:47,269
...that we were heading for a major--

1028
01:04:47,440 --> 01:04:50,398
This was all a process,
a gradual transformation...

1029
01:04:50,560 --> 01:04:55,634
...that we're kind of forcing the audience
to experience along with Veronica.

1030
01:04:55,800 --> 01:04:57,598
And...

1031
01:04:57,960 --> 01:05:00,520
But it had to have meaning.
It had to have narrative meaning.

1032
01:05:00,680 --> 01:05:04,560
And it had to have some sense
and cohesion of the kind...

1033
01:05:04,720 --> 01:05:07,838
...that I didn't find in
the original movie of The Fly.

1034
01:05:08,000 --> 01:05:11,755
I wanted it to all have
a weird scientific logic...

1035
01:05:11,920 --> 01:05:14,912
...underneath
the horrible emotional logic.

1036
01:05:15,080 --> 01:05:16,912
This. So it's scenes like that.

1037
01:05:20,240 --> 01:05:23,949
Knowing how a fly works,
how a fly digests its food...

1038
01:05:24,120 --> 01:05:27,351
...how completely different insects
are from humans...

1039
01:05:27,560 --> 01:05:29,471
...and yet somehow connected.

1040
01:05:29,640 --> 01:05:30,710
BRUNDLE:
Oh.

1041
01:05:30,960 --> 01:05:32,280
That's disgusting.

1042
01:05:37,680 --> 01:05:40,069
COMMENTARY:
Oh, this is kind of the operatic version...

1043
01:05:40,240 --> 01:05:42,151
...of anybody's disease, basically.

1044
01:05:42,600 --> 01:05:43,795
BRUNDLE:
My...

1045
01:05:44,600 --> 01:05:46,432
...ear. No!

1046
01:05:48,600 --> 01:05:51,479
COMMENTARY: This moment, I mean,
this is where the audience screamed.

1047
01:05:51,640 --> 01:05:54,029
And I think this is where
we had a lot of people fainting...

1048
01:05:54,280 --> 01:06:00,390
...was when Geena, or Veronica,
actually hugs Seth...

1049
01:06:00,560 --> 01:06:04,872
...in this condition, and puts her face
up to where his ear used to be.

1050
01:06:05,080 --> 01:06:07,435
That really got a lot of screams,
I have to say.

1051
01:06:07,600 --> 01:06:10,319
And in a way, it was unexpected,
but wonderful.

1052
01:06:10,480 --> 01:06:11,709
VERONICA:
That's your advice?

1053
01:06:11,960 --> 01:06:15,078
It could be contagious.
It could become an epidemic.

1054
01:06:16,240 --> 01:06:18,311
COMMENTARY:
And I really liked what happened here...

1055
01:06:18,480 --> 01:06:21,154
...was that
I've deliberately cut down...

1056
01:06:21,320 --> 01:06:23,880
...the human frames of reference
that all the characters have.

1057
01:06:24,040 --> 01:06:29,114
I mean, there are no mothers, brothers,
sisters, families, relatives.

1058
01:06:29,280 --> 01:06:33,956
So Veronica has to go back
to her old boyfriend for support...

1059
01:06:34,120 --> 01:06:38,239
...who's also, of course, her employer.
But it is really a triangle.

1060
01:06:38,400 --> 01:06:41,074
The movie really is a triangle.

1061
01:06:41,240 --> 01:06:44,790
And in that sense, it's very schematic.

1062
01:06:45,280 --> 01:06:48,272
Of course, people don't only know
two other people...

1063
01:06:48,440 --> 01:06:51,956
...especially if they're normal people
out in the world, working.

1064
01:06:54,560 --> 01:06:56,358
STATHIS:
Look, how about this?

1065
01:06:57,280 --> 01:06:59,237
COMMENTARY:
But I wanted her to be forced...

1066
01:06:59,400 --> 01:07:01,516
...to go back to Stathis...

1067
01:07:01,720 --> 01:07:04,109
...because of the sparks that there were
between them...

1068
01:07:04,280 --> 01:07:07,272
...and then because
of the emotional motivation...

1069
01:07:07,480 --> 01:07:10,757
...he would have
to risk his life to help her.

1070
01:07:10,920 --> 01:07:13,116
Not to mention that...

1071
01:07:14,000 --> 01:07:16,719
...he had mixed feelings,
of course, about...

1072
01:07:17,800 --> 01:07:22,397
...the deterioration of his great rival
for Veronica's love.

1073
01:07:26,200 --> 01:07:29,318
At one point, I think--
We did actually shoot a scene...

1074
01:07:29,480 --> 01:07:33,951
...that would end the movie
where she was living with Stathis...

1075
01:07:34,160 --> 01:07:36,993
...having married him,
got out of bed with him.

1076
01:07:37,200 --> 01:07:40,670
Audiences really hated that, and I had
to say I didn't like it very much myself.

1077
01:07:40,840 --> 01:07:43,116
They just didn't wanna see that.

1078
01:07:43,840 --> 01:07:45,160
But you never know.

1079
01:07:45,320 --> 01:07:47,630
That might have been
where Veronica ended up.

1080
01:07:47,920 --> 01:07:48,955
VERONICA:
Seth?

1081
01:07:49,200 --> 01:07:51,191
BRUNDLE:
No, no, no. Up here.

1082
01:07:53,880 --> 01:07:56,030
COMMENTARY:
Now, here is something that I'm sure...

1083
01:07:56,200 --> 01:07:58,714
...would've been done completely
by computer today...

1084
01:07:58,880 --> 01:08:00,917
...but was a completely physical effect.

1085
01:08:02,640 --> 01:08:06,474
It required building
a huge Ferris wheel.

1086
01:08:06,640 --> 01:08:10,031
The room was a Ferris wheel
that rotated.

1087
01:08:10,400 --> 01:08:13,313
So that when the camera
was upside down...

1088
01:08:13,480 --> 01:08:18,509
...and as Brundle crawls down the walls,
the set is rotating.

1089
01:08:18,680 --> 01:08:21,718
So he's really on the floor.

1090
01:08:21,880 --> 01:08:25,191
You know, it's gravity.
It's just gravity is doing the whole trick.

1091
01:08:25,400 --> 01:08:27,038
But...

1092
01:08:27,200 --> 01:08:31,239
...the set is revolving
as we shoot that shot.

1093
01:08:31,440 --> 01:08:35,274
And Mark Irwin, my director
of photography for this movie...

1094
01:08:35,480 --> 01:08:37,312
...worked out
a very ingenious system...

1095
01:08:37,480 --> 01:08:40,552
...for lighting inside that Ferris wheel,
which involved...

1096
01:08:40,720 --> 01:08:44,634
...bouncing light off mirrors and so on,
because there wasn't much throw.

1097
01:08:44,800 --> 01:08:47,076
There wasn't much distance
that you could get from...

1098
01:08:48,480 --> 01:08:53,077
From the set and from the actor,
in order to light it properly.

1099
01:08:53,240 --> 01:08:57,518
So there was a whole system of mirrors
and lighting through mirrors.

1100
01:08:57,680 --> 01:09:00,240
And all of that rotated with the set.

1101
01:09:00,400 --> 01:09:01,879
And it was a huge set.

1102
01:09:02,040 --> 01:09:06,398
It was quite an engineering feat to build
with the bearings that it had and so on.

1103
01:09:06,640 --> 01:09:10,554
I remember watching Ingmar Bergman's
film Hour of the Wolf...

1104
01:09:10,720 --> 01:09:13,997
...which had a scene where someone
is so grief-stricken, he walks up a wall.

1105
01:09:14,160 --> 01:09:18,154
But that was done very simply,
with no camera movement...

1106
01:09:18,360 --> 01:09:20,078
...and not following a moving actor.

1107
01:09:20,280 --> 01:09:22,112
So it was really a question of...

1108
01:09:22,320 --> 01:09:24,709
...building a floor
that looked like a ceiling...

1109
01:09:24,920 --> 01:09:26,593
...and shooting down onto it.

1110
01:09:26,800 --> 01:09:29,360
I remember looking at that movie,
though. A brilliant movie.

1111
01:09:30,200 --> 01:09:32,669
I'm a big Bergman fan.

1112
01:09:32,840 --> 01:09:37,118
Looking to it for guidance,
but it was done very starkly.

1113
01:09:37,280 --> 01:09:39,669
And it was very effective,
but it wouldn't have worked...

1114
01:09:39,880 --> 01:09:41,473
...for what we needed in this movie.

1115
01:09:43,640 --> 01:09:45,597
BRUNDLE:
How does Brundlefly eat?

1116
01:09:45,800 --> 01:09:47,154
He found out the hard way...

1117
01:09:47,320 --> 01:09:49,675
COMMENTARY: We actually looked at
a Fred Astaire movie...

1118
01:09:49,840 --> 01:09:51,513
...I think it was called
Royal Wedding...

1119
01:09:51,680 --> 01:09:55,560
...in which he dances up from the floor
to the wall to the ceiling.

1120
01:09:55,720 --> 01:09:58,075
And they used a revolving set then
in that movie.

1121
01:09:59,840 --> 01:10:03,959
We really just replicated
some very old technology for that one.

1122
01:10:05,520 --> 01:10:07,079
BRUNDLE:
He regurgitates on his food.

1123
01:10:07,280 --> 01:10:08,429
It liquefies.

1124
01:10:08,800 --> 01:10:11,155
And then he sucks it back up.

1125
01:10:11,800 --> 01:10:13,837
Ready for a demonstration, kids?

1126
01:10:14,560 --> 01:10:15,880
Here it goes.

1127
01:10:16,080 --> 01:10:17,275
[BRUNDLE REGURGITATES]

1128
01:10:17,480 --> 01:10:19,676
Oh, my God.

1129
01:10:21,240 --> 01:10:22,833
My God!

1130
01:10:25,240 --> 01:10:26,674
[DOOR OPENS]

1131
01:10:28,000 --> 01:10:29,832
COMMENTARY:
And once again, the whole idea...

1132
01:10:30,000 --> 01:10:31,229
...of documenting your life...

1133
01:10:31,400 --> 01:10:33,596
...no matter how hideous...

1134
01:10:33,760 --> 01:10:36,752
...in fact, the more hideous the moment,
the more you wanna document it.

1135
01:10:36,920 --> 01:10:40,390
And in a way,
it takes you out of your life.

1136
01:10:40,560 --> 01:10:43,712
It makes it a movie.
It makes it not be real.

1137
01:10:43,880 --> 01:10:46,554
And I think that's why people
have the impulse...

1138
01:10:46,720 --> 01:10:49,360
...to document their own lives now.

1139
01:10:49,520 --> 01:10:53,195
Even their worst moments.
Or especially their worst moments.

1140
01:10:53,520 --> 01:10:54,919
VERONICA:
I'm pregnant.

1141
01:10:56,080 --> 01:10:57,832
Oh, no.

1142
01:11:01,040 --> 01:11:02,439
Oh, no.

1143
01:11:05,680 --> 01:11:07,512
COMMENTARY:
Now, here we're into territory...

1144
01:11:07,720 --> 01:11:09,233
...that there was not...

1145
01:11:09,400 --> 01:11:13,633
...in the original movie of The Fly,
of course, and the short story.

1146
01:11:13,800 --> 01:11:17,714
But it's just a natural evolution...

1147
01:11:17,880 --> 01:11:23,034
...of a sexual relationship
and a love affair.

1148
01:11:24,800 --> 01:11:26,438
VERONICA:
I just...

1149
01:11:26,640 --> 01:11:28,074
I don't know.

1150
01:11:47,840 --> 01:11:49,558
COMMENTARY:
Gradually, Toronto has become...

1151
01:11:49,760 --> 01:11:52,673
...just a series of sets and locations...

1152
01:11:52,840 --> 01:11:55,832
...that are in my movies, to me.

1153
01:11:56,000 --> 01:11:58,799
Every time I turn a corner,
it's another place that I've shot...

1154
01:11:58,960 --> 01:12:00,792
...some scenes in a movie.

1155
01:12:00,960 --> 01:12:02,598
And I like that, actually.

1156
01:12:04,480 --> 01:12:08,758
VERONICA: Why am I losing it?
-It's better this way. You'll see.

1157
01:12:09,840 --> 01:12:12,070
It's the best thing that could happen.

1158
01:12:13,520 --> 01:12:15,670
COMMENTARY:
It's true I have a cameo in this scene...

1159
01:12:15,840 --> 01:12:19,071
...and it wasn't something
that I wanted.

1160
01:12:19,240 --> 01:12:21,914
And it's not something that I do.

1161
01:12:22,080 --> 01:12:25,152
I always prefer to get
better actors than me in my movies.

1162
01:12:25,360 --> 01:12:29,638
But there I am, with those very hideous
'80s glasses that were so popular.

1163
01:12:29,840 --> 01:12:34,152
And Geena asked me to do this role...

1164
01:12:34,320 --> 01:12:37,199
...because she just felt
she wanted me there.

1165
01:12:37,360 --> 01:12:40,955
If anybody was gonna be in that position
when she was up in the stirrups...

1166
01:12:41,120 --> 01:12:42,997
...she would rather it be her director.

1167
01:12:43,160 --> 01:12:45,276
So I said I would do it,
knowing that, of course...

1168
01:12:45,480 --> 01:12:48,632
...my face would be covered, and then
I could get somebody to dub my lines...

1169
01:12:48,800 --> 01:12:51,633
...if I wasn't happy
with my performance.

1170
01:12:51,800 --> 01:12:55,634
But I thought I did well enough,
so I left them in.

1171
01:12:57,080 --> 01:12:59,230
But those glasses. Oh, well.

1172
01:13:00,520 --> 01:13:03,080
Aesthetics change, don't they?

1173
01:13:03,560 --> 01:13:05,119
VERONICA:
No!

1174
01:13:05,280 --> 01:13:07,078
[VERONICA SCREAMING]

1175
01:13:17,440 --> 01:13:20,273
COMMENTARY: Once again, of course,
an incredibly potent nightmare.

1176
01:13:20,440 --> 01:13:25,196
The idea that you'll give birth
to something monstrous.

1177
01:13:27,360 --> 01:13:33,914
Whether you're an artist or a human
or a woman who is of childbearing age...

1178
01:13:34,080 --> 01:13:38,597
...this is a-- There's a lot
of very potent fears...

1179
01:13:38,760 --> 01:13:40,080
...touched on in this movie.

1180
01:13:40,240 --> 01:13:42,277
And that's really the strength
of the horror film.

1181
01:13:43,360 --> 01:13:45,954
That is the strength of the genre,
that it doesn't...

1182
01:13:46,120 --> 01:13:47,918
It cuts right to the chase, as they say.

1183
01:13:48,080 --> 01:13:54,679
It really deals with primal fears
very directly.

1184
01:13:55,080 --> 01:13:58,835
And that's obviously part
of the enduring popularity...

1185
01:13:59,000 --> 01:14:01,071
...of horror tales
that have always existed.

1186
01:14:01,240 --> 01:14:03,038
That it's...

1187
01:14:03,200 --> 01:14:08,479
If you can somehow have something
be even worse than what's in your life...

1188
01:14:08,640 --> 01:14:11,393
...it's somehow comforting.

1189
01:14:12,720 --> 01:14:16,236
Going further than even
your worst nightmare.

1190
01:14:16,400 --> 01:14:18,471
And that's what good horror films
give you.

1191
01:14:18,640 --> 01:14:21,632
And that's what The Fly
gave a lot of people as well.

1192
01:14:23,440 --> 01:14:25,511
Here's why I wanted
voice recognition...

1193
01:14:25,680 --> 01:14:28,513
...and why speech was so important.
Because...

1194
01:14:28,720 --> 01:14:32,998
...the idea that Brundle
should lose his voice...

1195
01:14:33,160 --> 01:14:35,356
...should lose his ability to articulate...

1196
01:14:35,520 --> 01:14:39,070
...10 me was a major moment
of horror and fear.

1197
01:14:41,320 --> 01:14:45,871
Which if you play it the way
the original was structured...

1198
01:14:46,040 --> 01:14:47,713
...wouldn't have ever been an issue.

1199
01:14:47,880 --> 01:14:50,679
One moment he can speak,
and the next minute he can't.

1200
01:14:50,840 --> 01:14:52,990
And that was it.
I wanted this also to be part...

1201
01:14:53,160 --> 01:14:57,950
...of the transition.
The ongoing deterioration.

1202
01:15:06,120 --> 01:15:12,116
And the idea of a little personal museum
of your former body parts...

1203
01:15:12,280 --> 01:15:14,112
...I think is even more relevant
these days...

1204
01:15:14,280 --> 01:15:16,476
...when so many people
do have former body parts...

1205
01:15:16,640 --> 01:15:18,836
...or are swapping body parts
with other people.

1206
01:15:19,000 --> 01:15:21,435
BRUNDLE:
Artifacts of a bygone era.

1207
01:15:23,320 --> 01:15:24,913
COMMENTARY:
It seems to be part...

1208
01:15:25,080 --> 01:15:28,357
...of the strange physical future
of the human race.

1209
01:15:32,360 --> 01:15:35,796
We'll all have these little museums
for ourselves...

1210
01:15:37,040 --> 01:15:39,475
...if we don't already.

1211
01:15:46,080 --> 01:15:48,356
BRUNDLE:
You've missed some good moments.

1212
01:15:49,400 --> 01:15:51,437
COMMENTARY:
Quite a few actors were approached...

1213
01:15:51,600 --> 01:15:53,079
...about playing this role...

1214
01:15:53,240 --> 01:15:59,430
...and most of them who said no
were afraid of the rubber.

1215
01:15:59,600 --> 01:16:01,750
They were afraid of the makeup.

1216
01:16:01,920 --> 01:16:03,831
They were afraid that
they would not be able...

1217
01:16:04,000 --> 01:16:07,516
...to act through the rubber,
that they would be lost.

1218
01:16:07,680 --> 01:16:09,990
And Jeff was totally not afraid.

1219
01:16:10,160 --> 01:16:12,754
On the contrary,
he was looking forward to it.

1220
01:16:12,920 --> 01:16:16,390
It was not even just a challenge,
it was something that he was...

1221
01:16:16,560 --> 01:16:18,995
He felt he could use,
and he wasn't afraid of it.

1222
01:16:19,160 --> 01:16:23,279
And that, you can see,
this would be the scene...

1223
01:16:23,440 --> 01:16:25,670
...that an actor would
either be intimidated...

1224
01:16:25,840 --> 01:16:29,276
...by what he had to wear, or not.

1225
01:16:29,440 --> 01:16:31,636
And Jeff, of course,
obviously was not.

1226
01:16:31,800 --> 01:16:34,679
And it required a lot of hours
in the makeup chair too.

1227
01:16:34,880 --> 01:16:39,113
I mean, it's quite a torturous
kind of thing to go through.

1228
01:16:39,280 --> 01:16:43,751
Starting very early in the morning, and
then starting five hours before that...

1229
01:16:43,920 --> 01:16:45,672
...in order to have
your makeup put on.

1230
01:16:45,840 --> 01:16:48,992
And being very uncomfortable
all the while.

1231
01:16:49,160 --> 01:16:51,117
I think Geena used to sing to Jeff...

1232
01:16:51,280 --> 01:16:54,989
...and read to him
while he was having his makeup put on.

1233
01:16:55,160 --> 01:16:56,798
BRUNDLE:
--and never come back here.

1234
01:17:01,640 --> 01:17:05,520
Have you heard of insect politics?

1235
01:17:07,600 --> 01:17:09,079
Neither have I.

1236
01:17:10,360 --> 01:17:12,158
COMMENTARY:
The insect-politics speech...

1237
01:17:12,320 --> 01:17:14,038
...has become somewhat famous
as well.

1238
01:17:16,440 --> 01:17:21,071
It's just my observation as, as I say,
a junior entomologist from an early age.

1239
01:17:21,240 --> 01:17:26,235
I was very fascinated by insect societies
like the kind that ants have.

1240
01:17:26,400 --> 01:17:29,836
There is a division of labor,
and they do attack...

1241
01:17:30,000 --> 01:17:32,230
...other ant colonies
and run off with slaves.

1242
01:17:32,400 --> 01:17:34,960
And they do have slaves,
and they do have...

1243
01:17:36,520 --> 01:17:40,593
...what they call ant cows.
They milk aphids for their honey.

1244
01:17:40,760 --> 01:17:44,674
So they have a very advanced
kind of social structure.

1245
01:17:44,840 --> 01:17:47,036
But they are still very, very much
not human...

1246
01:17:47,240 --> 01:17:50,710
...and that's really what
I was trying to get at with this speech.

1247
01:17:55,160 --> 01:17:58,152
And once again, Brundle is aware of it.

1248
01:17:58,320 --> 01:18:01,073
He's aware of what is happening to him,
and he can articulate it.

1249
01:18:01,240 --> 01:18:05,199
And to me, that makes it more poignant
and more tragic...

1250
01:18:05,360 --> 01:18:10,389
...and also more emotionally effective
and involving for the audience.

1251
01:18:10,600 --> 01:18:12,238
BRUNDLE:
--and the insect is awake.

1252
01:18:13,800 --> 01:18:15,359
No, Seth.

1253
01:18:17,080 --> 01:18:18,912
I'm saying...

1254
01:18:21,600 --> 01:18:23,910
...I'll hurt you if you stay.

1255
01:18:46,400 --> 01:18:49,472
[GROANING]

1256
01:18:49,720 --> 01:18:51,199
COMMENTARY:
Now, who knows how this...

1257
01:18:51,360 --> 01:18:53,351
...would have been done
in the modern era.

1258
01:18:53,720 --> 01:18:55,870
You know, certainly, there's a man
in a rubber suit playing the scene.

1259
01:18:56,040 --> 01:18:58,190
And it's obvious.

1260
01:18:59,520 --> 01:19:01,557
Of course, there are many ways
to do that now...

1261
01:19:01,720 --> 01:19:03,677
...where you might have
the actor acting...

1262
01:19:03,840 --> 01:19:07,276
...a completely
computer-generated character.

1263
01:19:08,520 --> 01:19:10,477
Although, doing the acting himself...

1264
01:19:10,640 --> 01:19:13,712
...would have been
more extreme and more effective...

1265
01:19:13,880 --> 01:19:19,353
...in the sense of body shape that
an actual human body could not have.

1266
01:19:19,840 --> 01:19:23,470
But there's a sort of an immediacy
and a physicality and a realness...

1267
01:19:24,280 --> 01:19:27,830
...to the man-in-the-rubber-suit
routine...

1268
01:19:28,000 --> 01:19:30,469
...that's very effective as well.

1269
01:19:30,680 --> 01:19:35,914
So I suppose it might, you know,
look very, hm, tacky...

1270
01:19:36,080 --> 01:19:38,674
...and a few other things
to a modern audience.

1271
01:19:38,840 --> 01:19:40,831
But at the same time...

1272
01:19:41,000 --> 01:19:43,560
...there's an immediacy
and a physical presence...

1273
01:19:43,720 --> 01:19:45,040
...that you don't get with CGI.

1274
01:19:45,200 --> 01:19:50,354
And I wonder in about 20 years' time
how something like this...

1275
01:19:50,520 --> 01:19:54,798
...will compare with the way
CGI looks to a future audience.

1276
01:19:54,960 --> 01:19:57,679
Because CGI also has its drawbacks...

1277
01:19:57,840 --> 01:20:00,912
...and it, too, might look quite tacky
and primitive in 20 years...

1278
01:20:02,640 --> 01:20:05,553
...or even fewer years than that.

1279
01:20:06,840 --> 01:20:09,639
DOCTOR:
Well, okay, what's the story?

1280
01:20:14,040 --> 01:20:17,078
COMMENTARY: This actor, Les Carlson,
I've worked with him in many movies...

1281
01:20:17,240 --> 01:20:18,594
...Videodrome in particular.

1282
01:20:18,760 --> 01:20:23,357
He's just a favorite Canadian actor
of mine I love to work with.

1283
01:20:26,560 --> 01:20:30,076
He plays Barry Convex,
the bad guy, in Videodrome.

1284
01:20:30,320 --> 01:20:31,435
STATHIS:
Please.

1285
01:20:33,720 --> 01:20:35,552
Is it your child?

1286
01:20:35,720 --> 01:20:36,755
No.

1287
01:20:40,160 --> 01:20:44,438
It's the child of a man
who is deformed.

1288
01:20:46,200 --> 01:20:47,634
I don't mean to interfere...

1289
01:20:47,880 --> 01:20:51,794
...but I detect a certain
uncertainty here.

1290
01:20:52,040 --> 01:20:54,919
There are tests we can do
to determine whether--

1291
01:20:55,160 --> 01:20:59,279
I don't want tests.
Tests can't guarantee anything.

1292
01:20:59,680 --> 01:21:02,832
The baby could start off normal,
then become...

1293
01:21:05,960 --> 01:21:07,439
I want an abortion.

1294
01:21:10,440 --> 01:21:12,636
I'll do it myself if I have to.

1295
01:21:13,760 --> 01:21:17,640
If you'll slip into that,
we'll be on our way in no time.

1296
01:21:17,800 --> 01:21:21,350
COMMENTARY: Once again,
this is another hot button, I suppose...

1297
01:21:21,520 --> 01:21:23,670
...and it's gotten hotter
since we made this movie.

1298
01:21:23,840 --> 01:21:28,676
That is, the idea of abortion,
and aborting a fetus...

1299
01:21:28,880 --> 01:21:31,872
...that might or might not be
monstrous or deformed...

1300
01:21:32,040 --> 01:21:34,839
...or defective in some way or other.

1301
01:21:37,400 --> 01:21:39,152
I was operating one of the cameras...

1302
01:21:39,320 --> 01:21:44,679
...that shot that jumping-through-
the-glass-blocks moment.

1303
01:21:44,840 --> 01:21:47,514
I don't normally like to operate...

1304
01:21:47,680 --> 01:21:50,354
...because you start to worry more about
your framing and so on...

1305
01:21:50,520 --> 01:21:52,352
...than the dramatic things
that are going on.

1306
01:21:52,520 --> 01:21:54,591
But I think we ran out of operators
at that point...

1307
01:21:54,760 --> 01:21:57,274
...So I operated one of the cameras.

1308
01:22:01,440 --> 01:22:03,795
That was a set
that we were just looking at...

1309
01:22:04,000 --> 01:22:07,709
...but this is really the rooftop
of the Bishop Strachan School...

1310
01:22:07,920 --> 01:22:12,198
...which is really around the corner
from my house.

1311
01:22:12,360 --> 01:22:15,671
A school where two
of my daughters attended.

1312
01:22:15,840 --> 01:22:17,160
Great rooftop.

1313
01:22:17,320 --> 01:22:19,914
BRUNDLE:
--want to kill Brundle?

1314
01:22:20,360 --> 01:22:25,275
The baby might be all that's left
of the real me.

1315
01:22:25,480 --> 01:22:26,993
COMMENTARY:
Once again, this is a scene...

1316
01:22:27,160 --> 01:22:30,039
...that could have been done in a studio
with green screen and so on...

1317
01:22:30,200 --> 01:22:32,396
...but we were actually up there
on that roof.

1318
01:22:33,160 --> 01:22:34,559
VERONICA:
I'm afraid.

1319
01:22:34,880 --> 01:22:38,589
Please, have the baby.

1320
01:22:39,360 --> 01:22:40,395
I can't.

1321
01:22:40,960 --> 01:22:42,314
I can't.

1322
01:22:42,480 --> 01:22:45,074
COMMENTARY:
The scenes like this are really, I found...

1323
01:22:45,240 --> 01:22:50,030
...very, very, very
emotionally wrenching, I have to say.

1324
01:22:50,200 --> 01:22:54,876
And I guess the argument for rubber
would be best with that scene.

1325
01:22:56,000 --> 01:22:58,992
You know, you see an actual face
and an actual person acting.

1326
01:22:59,160 --> 01:23:03,040
You know it's not drawn
and it's not drawn by computer.

1327
01:23:03,200 --> 01:23:05,396
That's a real person there.

1328
01:23:05,600 --> 01:23:08,752
And the emotion
really does come through.

1329
01:23:19,000 --> 01:23:20,593
Now we have the good Stathis,
you see?

1330
01:23:20,760 --> 01:23:26,153
He's heroic,
and he's come to rescue his woman.

1331
01:23:30,040 --> 01:23:32,350
And I think it was implicit all the time.

1332
01:23:32,520 --> 01:23:35,319
You really suddenly realize...

1333
01:23:35,480 --> 01:23:37,391
...that he wasn't really
the bad guy after all.

1334
01:23:37,560 --> 01:23:40,996
He was another part of a real triangle.

1335
01:23:43,720 --> 01:23:46,439
But, of course, he's out of his depth.

1336
01:24:01,480 --> 01:24:05,474
This shotgun, of course,
is not a combat shotgun.

1337
01:24:05,640 --> 01:24:07,756
It's really for skeet shooting
or bird shooting.

1338
01:24:07,920 --> 01:24:09,399
It's not really...

1339
01:24:09,560 --> 01:24:12,518
It's a kind of an effete,
rather pretty, elegant gun.

1340
01:24:12,680 --> 01:24:14,512
Not good for fly hunting, really.

1341
01:24:21,520 --> 01:24:24,273
Even props
are expressive of character.

1342
01:24:24,480 --> 01:24:28,155
And getting the design
of the props right is a real art.

1343
01:24:48,040 --> 01:24:51,032
Once again, a computer
has to make it perfectly clear.

1344
01:25:09,200 --> 01:25:10,793
[SCREECHES]

1345
01:25:14,520 --> 01:25:15,999
Jesus!

1346
01:25:19,520 --> 01:25:20,555
Ah!

1347
01:25:23,880 --> 01:25:26,440
COMMENTARY:
We did have a complete...

1348
01:25:26,600 --> 01:25:28,910
...Brundlefly puppet...

1349
01:25:29,320 --> 01:25:34,110
...for those moments of vomiting
of fly fluid.

1350
01:25:35,040 --> 01:25:37,395
You'll see him again.

1351
01:25:59,480 --> 01:26:01,551
And this is him.

1352
01:26:01,720 --> 01:26:05,190
A puppet being operated
by about 20 guys under the floor...

1353
01:26:05,360 --> 01:26:07,636
...as they usually do.

1354
01:26:09,800 --> 01:26:11,518
There's Jeff.

1355
01:26:24,840 --> 01:26:28,151
VERONICA:
No, don't. Don't, please.

1356
01:26:28,520 --> 01:26:29,840
Please.

1357
01:26:33,400 --> 01:26:35,710
COMMENTARY:
A lot of literary reverberations.

1358
01:26:35,880 --> 01:26:38,679
You know, you've certainly got
Beauty and the Beast happening here.

1359
01:26:38,840 --> 01:26:41,593
A lot of force,
but ultimately behind the story...

1360
01:26:41,760 --> 01:26:47,312
...in terms of mythology and archetype,
I suppose.

1361
01:26:48,200 --> 01:26:50,760
We're in the rotating set again
with this shot.

1362
01:26:52,760 --> 01:26:54,034
BRUNDLE:
Help me.

1363
01:26:54,840 --> 01:26:55,875
Help me be human.

1364
01:26:57,720 --> 01:26:59,040
How?

1365
01:27:06,840 --> 01:27:08,194
Well...

1366
01:27:12,240 --> 01:27:14,390
COMMENTARY:
And once again...

1367
01:27:14,600 --> 01:27:17,240
...like an old couple
who can't really experience anything...

1368
01:27:17,480 --> 01:27:19,232
...unless they share it
with each other...

1369
01:27:19,480 --> 01:27:23,075
...Brundle just simply wants to share.

1370
01:27:24,160 --> 01:27:28,996
And of course, there's a kind of a
Frankenstein reverb there as well.

1371
01:27:29,200 --> 01:27:33,159
The idea that Frankenstein
wants a monster wife.

1372
01:27:36,160 --> 01:27:37,195
BRUNDLE:
Together.

1373
01:27:54,600 --> 01:27:55,635
No...

1374
01:27:56,680 --> 01:27:58,876
We'll be the ultimate family.

1375
01:27:59,360 --> 01:28:03,752
A family of three,
joined together in one body.

1376
01:28:05,520 --> 01:28:07,158
COMMENTARY:
Jeff had to learn to speak...

1377
01:28:07,360 --> 01:28:09,590
...with various kinds
of prosthetic teeth.

1378
01:28:10,360 --> 01:28:14,240
He had to have a speech therapist
work with him.

1379
01:28:15,320 --> 01:28:16,640
Of course, now he stops talking.

1380
01:28:16,800 --> 01:28:21,397
But you see how late in the movie
the talking is over.

1381
01:28:23,440 --> 01:28:25,477
Because there's not much
of the movie left to go...

1382
01:28:25,640 --> 01:28:28,359
...80 it was all right for him
to lose it, finally...

1383
01:28:28,520 --> 01:28:30,352
...for his final transformation.

1384
01:28:30,520 --> 01:28:33,273
But yes, he had to learn how
to continue to speak...

1385
01:28:33,440 --> 01:28:37,513
...with various prosthetic teeth,
which were a part of his transformation.

1386
01:28:40,080 --> 01:28:43,710
And develop the sort of twitching,
the fly twitching...

1387
01:28:43,920 --> 01:28:47,470
...without it being too much,
and without it being over-the-top.

1388
01:28:47,840 --> 01:28:50,593
VERONICA:
Oh, God, please!

1389
01:29:18,360 --> 01:29:21,876
COMMENTARY: And, of course,
this is a full-sized puppet.

1390
01:29:22,040 --> 01:29:23,439
Huge.

1391
01:29:23,600 --> 01:29:28,231
And beautifully operated.
And so is this.

1392
01:29:28,440 --> 01:29:31,558
These are things that I think now
would definitely be done CGI...

1393
01:29:31,720 --> 01:29:34,394
...and they would have
more fluidity and so on.

1394
01:29:34,560 --> 01:29:37,791
But they might not quite have
the physical presence.

1395
01:29:38,000 --> 01:29:39,911
So you gain things
and you lose things.

1396
01:29:40,120 --> 01:29:41,713
If you've watched an insect...

1397
01:29:41,880 --> 01:29:44,110
...you know that they do move
like little puppets.

1398
01:29:44,280 --> 01:29:48,956
I mean, they-- There can be a kind of
puppetlike motion to both rodents...

1399
01:29:49,120 --> 01:29:52,954
...like rats and squirrels, and insects.

1400
01:29:53,120 --> 01:29:56,192
So that was very possible
to replicate that...

1401
01:29:56,360 --> 01:30:00,274
...with the motion that you see there
with this puppet.

1402
01:30:04,160 --> 01:30:06,754
Now, this fly-fusion creature...

1403
01:30:06,920 --> 01:30:11,437
...definitely does not have
the eyes of an insect.

1404
01:30:11,640 --> 01:30:15,520
Maybe if I'm the kid in the audience,
this is what I'm gonna complain about.

1405
01:30:15,680 --> 01:30:17,591
But I wanted it, of course, to have...

1406
01:30:17,760 --> 01:30:20,513
...some human expressiveness
for the emotion.

1407
01:30:20,680 --> 01:30:22,512
And I guess you could justify it
by saying:

1408
01:30:22,680 --> 01:30:26,719
"Well, it got Brundle's eyes, even if
it didn't get the rest of his body."

1409
01:30:26,920 --> 01:30:31,869
So I went for emotional eyes
on this creature.

1410
01:30:32,040 --> 01:30:33,519
I thought they were very important...

1411
01:30:33,680 --> 01:30:35,876
...and, of course, you'll see soon
why we needed them.

1412
01:30:38,760 --> 01:30:40,956
They're kind of a big version
of Jeff's eyes...

1413
01:30:41,120 --> 01:30:45,990
...with a little bit of a green tint
to give you the insect aspect.

1414
01:30:46,160 --> 01:30:51,758
But insect eyes are not expressive, and
I really needed expressiveness now...

1415
01:30:51,920 --> 01:30:54,912
...because we no longer had
the articulation.

1416
01:30:55,080 --> 01:30:57,071
He could no longer speak, of course.

1417
01:30:57,280 --> 01:30:59,794
So the eyes were all that was left.

1418
01:31:04,800 --> 01:31:06,677
Because Brundle, of course...

1419
01:31:06,840 --> 01:31:09,878
...has to make it clear
that he wants to die.

1420
01:31:10,040 --> 01:31:12,793
And he can't say it.

1421
01:31:12,960 --> 01:31:15,759
So he has to use his eyes.

1422
01:31:20,320 --> 01:31:26,589
There were many possible codas
that we thought of for the movie...

1423
01:31:27,480 --> 01:31:31,030
...that dealt with Veronica giving birth.

1424
01:31:31,200 --> 01:31:35,956
Or not giving birth. Giving birth
to a perfectly healthy baby...

1425
01:31:36,120 --> 01:31:38,999
...giving birth to a butterfly baby,
all kinds of--

1426
01:31:39,200 --> 01:31:41,350
Or maybe that was a dream.

1427
01:31:41,520 --> 01:31:46,230
But we found that the ending
was so shattering for the audience...

1428
01:31:46,440 --> 01:31:49,512
...that they-- Nothing really worked.
I mean, there was really--

1429
01:31:49,680 --> 01:31:53,275
They didn't really wanna hear or see
anything after this.

1430
01:31:54,200 --> 01:31:57,955
And it's funny, because I had
the same structural situation...

1431
01:31:58,120 --> 01:32:00,270
...on The Dead Zone,
which ends just like this.

1432
01:32:00,440 --> 01:32:02,829
I mean, it's the same ending
as The Dead Zone, basically.

1433
01:32:03,000 --> 01:32:07,870
Although in this case, the woman
actually does the mercy killing.

1434
01:32:10,760 --> 01:32:12,194
There were many attempts to--

1435
01:32:12,400 --> 01:32:14,198
Or several attempts
on The Dead Zone also...

1436
01:32:14,400 --> 01:32:17,392
...to have a kind of coda that dealt
with the characters who were left...

1437
01:32:17,560 --> 01:32:20,518
...after the Chris Walken character dies
in The Dead Zone.

1438
01:32:20,680 --> 01:32:23,354
I hope I haven't spoiled it for you
if you haven't seen it.

1439
01:32:23,520 --> 01:32:26,034
But none of them worked...

1440
01:32:26,200 --> 01:32:29,875
...and it just seemed better to just
get out when the movie was over...

1441
01:32:30,080 --> 01:32:34,074
...rather than try to deal with something
that just wasn't gonna work.

1442
01:32:34,240 --> 01:32:35,992
So it occurred to me...

1443
01:32:36,400 --> 01:32:38,835
...when we were deciding what to do
about the ending here...

1444
01:32:39,000 --> 01:32:42,675
...that it would probably be best to just
do what we did with The Dead Zone...

1445
01:32:43,120 --> 01:32:45,953
...which was to leave it
with the death of the main character...

1446
01:32:46,120 --> 01:32:48,350
...and that was the end of the movie.

1447
01:32:52,720 --> 01:32:57,510
The question of technology and science
and morality and ethics...

1448
01:32:57,680 --> 01:33:00,149
...is often raised in my movies.

1449
01:33:00,360 --> 01:33:03,034
And there has been, of course,
the kind of...

1450
01:33:03,240 --> 01:33:07,234
The Romantic concept of going too far.
That you cannot assault the gods.

1451
01:33:08,040 --> 01:33:09,872
If you fly too close to the sun...

1452
01:33:10,080 --> 01:33:12,117
...the wax
holding your wings together melts...

1453
01:33:12,280 --> 01:33:14,476
...and you plunge to your doom.

1454
01:33:14,640 --> 01:33:16,950
I don't really believe in destiny per se.

1455
01:33:17,120 --> 01:33:22,194
But I think that it is innate in our nature
to constantly change everything...

1456
01:33:22,360 --> 01:33:25,159
...to question everything,
to try to understand everything.

1457
01:33:25,320 --> 01:33:29,837
And it will inevitably create
good and bad.

1458
01:33:30,000 --> 01:33:32,116
And I don't think
there's any stopping it.

1459
01:33:32,280 --> 01:33:35,955
And, of course, now that we've sort of
dived into the plasma pool, literally...

1460
01:33:36,120 --> 01:33:40,034
...that we are dealing with DNA,
and the double helix...

1461
01:33:40,200 --> 01:33:42,874
...and the actual structure of life itself
and the human body...

1462
01:33:43,040 --> 01:33:45,759
...and the question of
human consciousness and so on...

1463
01:33:45,920 --> 01:33:48,878
...it's a Pandora's box,
in the sense that it has been opened...

1464
01:33:49,080 --> 01:33:51,151
...and it will not be closed.
There's no question.

1465
01:33:51,320 --> 01:33:56,394
So I think we have to just accept
that that is what our destiny is...

1466
01:33:56,560 --> 01:33:59,871
...to do all those things. To change
everything. To try to control everything.

1467
01:34:00,040 --> 01:34:02,600
And also to fail at it
in terms of the big picture.

1468
01:34:02,800 --> 01:34:06,156
I think it's a constant struggling,
a constant striving.

1469
01:34:06,320 --> 01:34:09,790
And it's true that
characters in my movies who do that...

1470
01:34:09,960 --> 01:34:12,395
...often come to terrible ends...

1471
01:34:12,600 --> 01:34:15,911
...but that has more, I think,
to do with the nature of drama...

1472
01:34:16,080 --> 01:34:18,993
...than it does
with the nature of philosophy.

1473
01:34:19,160 --> 01:34:21,310
It has to do with the fact that...

1474
01:34:21,480 --> 01:34:24,632
...as George Bernard Shaw said,
"Conflict is the essence of drama.”

1475
01:34:24,800 --> 01:34:27,519
So if you have someone
who comes up with a great invention...

1476
01:34:27,680 --> 01:34:29,671
...or a great cure for a disease...

1477
01:34:29,840 --> 01:34:32,036
...and it works
and that's the end of it...

1478
01:34:32,200 --> 01:34:33,838
...then it's not much of a drama.

1479
01:34:34,040 --> 01:34:36,554
So it's much more
interesting dramatically...

1480
01:34:36,720 --> 01:34:40,429
...and it's also more illuminating,
when things go wrong.

1481
01:34:40,600 --> 01:34:43,069
It's when things go wrong
that you understand...

1482
01:34:43,240 --> 01:34:45,311
...the nature of what happens
when things go right.

1483
01:34:45,480 --> 01:34:50,270
So I'm not really trying to promote
a conservative approach to science...

1484
01:34:50,440 --> 01:34:52,397
...or to art at all.

1485
01:34:52,600 --> 01:34:54,591
And I think you can see, too...

1486
01:34:54,760 --> 01:34:57,559
...that even with what Brundle
undergoes in this movie...

1487
01:34:57,720 --> 01:34:59,119
...there is an exhilaration.

1488
01:34:59,280 --> 01:35:01,476
There is an incredible experience
that he has.

1489
01:35:01,640 --> 01:35:03,472
And though he ends up dying...

1490
01:35:03,640 --> 01:35:05,677
...one can say,
"Well, we all end up dying...

1491
01:35:05,840 --> 01:35:10,152
...and perhaps it's more interesting,
more exciting...

1492
01:35:10,320 --> 01:35:12,470
...more thrilling, a better way to live...

1493
01:35:12,640 --> 01:35:14,790
...to have gone through
an amazing transformation...

1494
01:35:15,000 --> 01:35:17,071
...an amazing adventure in your life."

1495
01:35:17,240 --> 01:35:20,631
To die young,
but to die young in an amazing way...

1496
01:35:20,800 --> 01:35:24,430
...having had a terrifically exciting
or provocative life.

1497
01:35:24,600 --> 01:35:27,274
It's a tossup, you know.
And I think that that flip of the coin...

1498
01:35:27,440 --> 01:35:30,956
...is where we are at as humans.

1499
01:35:34,640 --> 01:35:36,631
[ENGLISH SDH]

